WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.020
How much air can your lungs hold?

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:06.551
Today we'll find out, using just a balloon and a ruler.

00:00:07.101 --> 00:00:08.967
We'll measure three lung volumes.

00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:10.169
Tidal volume

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:12.181
Expiratory reserve volume

00:00:12.231 --> 00:00:13.772
And vital capacity

00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:17.303
Each one tells us something different about how we breathe.

00:00:17.953 --> 00:00:20.141
One safety note before we start.

00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:25.302
Anyone with asthma or another breathing difficulty should not collect their own data.

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:28.865
If that's you, please talk with your teacher before you begin.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.446
First, stretch the balloon five times.

00:00:33.496 --> 00:00:35.544
Pull it wide, then let it relax.

00:00:35.694 --> 00:00:36.535
One.

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:37.801
Two.

00:00:38.251 --> 00:00:39.168
Three.

00:00:39.618 --> 00:00:40.549
Four.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:41.935
Five.

00:00:42.385 --> 00:00:45.263
That loosens the rubber, so it's easier to blow up.

00:00:45.713 --> 00:00:49.805
Now lay the balloon flat on the table, and stand the ruler up beside it.

00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:53.111
Set the zero end of the ruler on the table.

00:00:53.761 --> 00:00:56.574
Keep the centimeter side of the ruler facing you.

00:00:57.224 --> 00:01:02.350
We measure the diameter: the distance straight across the balloon at its widest part.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.250
Read the ruler at the top of the balloon. This one is about 8 centimeters.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.567
Hold still for a moment, and read it carefully.
